2
00:00:00,960 --> 00:00:07,909
This is software engineering radio, the podcast for professional developers on the web at se-radio.net.

3
00:00:07,919 --> 00:00:12,070
SE radio is brought to you by the ITE computer society.

4
00:00:12,070 --> 00:00:15,790
and by it e software magazine online at computer.org/software.

5
00:00:15,800 --> 00:00:19,109
[Music]

6
00:00:19,119 --> 00:00:26,390
Hey, this is Jeremy Jung for Software Engineering Radio and today I'm talking to Elizabeth Fagiraa.

7
00:00:26,390 --> 00:00:28,470
She's a wine developer at Codew Weavers.

8
00:00:28,470 --> 00:00:32,150
and today we're going to talk about what that is and all the work that goes into it.

9
00:00:32,160 --> 00:00:34,470
Elizabeth, welcome to Software Engineering Radio.

10
00:00:34,480 --> 00:00:36,631
Thank you Jeremy. I'm glad to be here.

11
00:00:36,640 --> 00:00:41,590
I think the first thing we should talk about is maybe saying what wine is.

12
00:00:41,600 --> 00:00:44,549
because I think a lot of people aren't familiar with the project.

13
00:00:44,559 --> 00:00:47,510
So Wine is a translation layer.

14
00:00:47,510 --> 00:00:49,590
In fact, I would say Wine is a Windows emulator.

15
00:00:49,600 --> 00:00:53,750
That is what the name originally stood for.

16
00:00:53,750 --> 00:00:57,270
It reimplements the entire Windows or you say wind32 API.

17
00:00:57,270 --> 00:01:02,069
so that programs that make calls into the API will then transfer that code to wine.

18
00:01:02,069 --> 00:01:07,510
and we allow that Windows programs to run on things that are not Windows.

19
00:01:07,510 --> 00:01:13,350
So Linux, Mac OS, other operating systems such as Solaris and BSD.

20
00:01:13,350 --> 00:01:17,510
It works not by emulating the CPU,

21
00:01:17,510 --> 00:01:21,270
but by re-implementing every API basically from scratch and translating them to their equivalent.

22
00:01:21,270 --> 00:01:23,990
or writing new code in case there is no equivalent.

23
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:29,109
I believe what you're doing is you're emulating system calls.

24
00:01:29,109 --> 00:01:34,230
Could you explain what those are and how that relates to the project?

25
00:01:34,230 --> 00:01:38,950
So system call in general can be used to refer to a call into the operating system to.

26
00:01:38,960 --> 00:01:42,789
execute some functionality that's built into the operating system.

27
00:01:42,789 --> 00:01:46,550
Often it's used in the context of talking to the kernel.

28
00:01:46,550 --> 00:01:48,230
Windows applications actually tend to talk at a much higher level.

29
00:01:48,240 --> 00:01:51,830
because there's so much highle functionality built into Windows.

30
00:01:51,830 --> 00:01:54,630
as opposed to other operating systems that.

31
00:01:54,640 --> 00:02:01,990
basically we end up implementing much higher level behavior than you would on Linux.

32
00:02:01,990 --> 00:02:03,990
And can you give some examples of what some of those system calls would be.

33
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:08,150
and I suppose how they may be higher level than some of the Linux ones?

34
00:02:08,160 --> 00:02:10,150
Sure.

35
00:02:10,150 --> 00:02:14,630
So of course you have low-level calls like interacting with a file system,

36
00:02:14,630 --> 00:02:18,630
create a file and read and write and such.

37
00:02:18,630 --> 00:02:20,470
You also have highle APIs to interact with a sound driver.

38
00:02:20,470 --> 00:02:25,350
There's one I was working on earlier today called X Audio.

39
00:02:25,350 --> 00:02:29,830
where you actually build this bank of sounds that's meant to be played in a game.

40
00:02:29,830 --> 00:02:32,470
and then you can position them in various 3D space.

41
00:02:32,470 --> 00:02:37,030
and the operating system in a sense will take care of all of the math.

42
00:02:37,030 --> 00:02:39,190
that goes into making that work.

43
00:02:39,190 --> 00:02:41,350
That's all running on your computer and.

44
00:02:41,350 --> 00:02:44,949
then it'll send that audio data to the sound card once it's transformed it.

45
00:02:44,949 --> 00:02:46,949
so it sounds like it's coming from a certain space.

46
00:02:46,959 --> 00:02:52,710
lot of other things like parsing XML is another big one.

47
00:02:52,710 --> 00:02:55,509
but there's a lot of things the space is honestly huge.

48
00:02:55,519 --> 00:03:01,911
yeah I can sort of see how those might be things you might not expect to be done. by the operating system.

49
00:03:01,911 --> 00:03:07,030
like you gave the example of 3D audio and XML parsing.

50
00:03:07,030 --> 00:03:10,949
and I think XML parsing in particular you would have thought that that would be something.

51
00:03:10,949 --> 00:03:16,070
that would be handled by the standard library of whatever language the person was writing their application is.

52
00:03:16,070 --> 00:03:18,149
so that's interesting that is built into the OS.

53
00:03:18,159 --> 00:03:21,190
Yeah. Well, in languages like C, it isn't even part of the standard library.

54
00:03:21,200 --> 00:03:22,790
It's higher level than that.

55
00:03:22,790 --> 00:03:29,350
It's you have specific libraries that are widespread but not codified in a standard.

56
00:03:29,350 --> 00:03:30,869
But in Windows, they are part of the operating system.

57
00:03:30,869 --> 00:03:36,070
And in fact, there's several different XML parsers in the operating system.

58
00:03:36,070 --> 00:03:40,390
Microsoft likes to deprecate old APIs and make new ones that do the same thing very often.

59
00:03:40,390 --> 00:03:47,270
And something I've heard about Windows is that they're typically very reluctant to break backwards compatibility.

60
00:03:47,270 --> 00:03:51,030
So you say they're deprecated, but do they typically keep all of them still in there?

61
00:03:51,040 --> 00:03:57,509
It all still works. And that's all things that Wine has to implement as well to make sure.

62
00:03:57,509 --> 00:03:59,351
that the software works as well. Yeah.

63
00:03:59,360 --> 00:04:01,110
Yeah.

64
00:04:01,110 --> 00:04:03,830
And we also need to implement those things to make old programs work.

65
00:04:03,840 --> 00:04:10,070
because there is a lot of demand at least from people using wine for getting some really old.

66
00:04:10,070 --> 00:04:12,470
programs working from the early '9s even.

67
00:04:12,480 --> 00:04:18,629
and that's probably a good thing to talk about in terms of what are the types of software.

68
00:04:18,629 --> 00:04:22,230
that people are trying to run with wine and what operating system are they typically using.

69
00:04:22,240 --> 00:04:24,950
in terms of software literally all kinds.

70
00:04:24,960 --> 00:04:31,350
any software you can imagine that runs on Windows people will try to run it on wine.

71
00:04:31,350 --> 00:04:35,270
So we're talking games, office software, productivity software, accounting.

72
00:04:35,270 --> 00:04:42,790
People will run build systems on wine just run builder programs using on visual studio running on wine.

73
00:04:42,790 --> 00:04:46,870
People will run wine on servers for example like software as a service kind of things.

74
00:04:46,870 --> 00:04:49,510
where you don't even know that it's running on wine.

75
00:04:49,510 --> 00:04:54,310
really super domain specific stuff like I've run astronomy software.

76
00:04:54,310 --> 00:04:59,110
and wine design computer system design even hardware drivers can sometimes work on wine.

77
00:04:59,120 --> 00:05:04,070
there's a bit of a gray area I think from maybe the general public.

78
00:05:04,070 --> 00:05:08,790
or at least from what I've seen I think a lot of people's exposure to it is for.

79
00:05:08,800 --> 00:05:12,230
playing games.

80
00:05:12,230 --> 00:05:17,110
is there something different about games versus all those other types of productivity software.

81
00:05:17,110 --> 00:05:20,790
and office software that makes supporting those different.

82
00:05:20,800 --> 00:05:23,909
There's some things about it that are different.

83
00:05:23,909 --> 00:05:25,749
Games, of course, have gotten a lot of publicity lately.

84
00:05:25,749 --> 00:05:30,629
because there's been a huge push largely from Valve,

85
00:05:30,629 --> 00:05:36,071
but also some other companies to get a lot a huge wide range of games working. well under wine.

86
00:05:36,071 --> 00:05:37,590
And that's really panned out in a way.

87
00:05:37,590 --> 00:05:39,430
I think we've largely succeeded.

88
00:05:39,430 --> 00:05:44,070
We've made huge strides in the past several years, 5 10 years, I think.

89
00:05:44,070 --> 00:05:47,749
So when you talk about what makes games different,

90
00:05:47,749 --> 00:05:51,670
I think one thing games tend to do is they have a very limited set of things they're.

91
00:05:51,680 --> 00:05:54,390
working with.

92
00:05:54,390 --> 00:05:57,990
and they often want to make things run fast.

93
00:05:57,990 --> 00:05:59,909
So they're working very close to the metal.

94
00:05:59,909 --> 00:06:02,390
They're not going to use an XML parser, for example.

95
00:06:02,390 --> 00:06:05,670
They're just going to talk as directly to the graphics driver as they can, right?

96
00:06:05,670 --> 00:06:09,510
And probably going to do all their own sound design.

97
00:06:09,510 --> 00:06:11,350
I did talk about that X audio library,

98
00:06:11,350 --> 00:06:15,590
but a lot of games will just talk as directly to the sound driver as Windows lets them.

99
00:06:15,590 --> 00:06:17,430
So, this is often a blessing honestly.

100
00:06:17,430 --> 00:06:20,870
because it means there's less we have to implement to make them work.

101
00:06:20,870 --> 00:06:22,629
When you look at a lot of productivity applications.

102
00:06:22,629 --> 00:06:29,830
and especially the other thing that makes some productivity applications harder is Microsoft makes them.

103
00:06:29,830 --> 00:06:34,629
and they like to make a library for use in this one program like Microsoft Office.

104
00:06:34,629 --> 00:06:39,510
and then say, well, other programs might use this as well.

105
00:06:39,510 --> 00:06:41,350
let's put it in the operating system.

106
00:06:41,350 --> 00:06:45,830
and expose it and write an API for it and everything and maybe some other programs use it.

107
00:06:45,830 --> 00:06:48,309
Mostly it's just Office, but it means.

108
00:06:48,309 --> 00:06:52,710
that Office relies on a lot of things from the operating system that we all have to reimplement.

109
00:06:52,720 --> 00:06:57,510
Yeah, that's somewhat counterintuitive because when you think of games,

110
00:06:57,510 --> 00:07:01,909
you think of these really high performance things that seem really complicated,

111
00:07:01,909 --> 00:07:07,270
but it sounds like from what you're saying because they use the lower level primitives,

112
00:07:07,270 --> 00:07:08,790
they're actually easier in some ways to support.

113
00:07:08,800 --> 00:07:14,230
Yeah, certainly in some ways they'll do things like reimplement the heap allocator.

114
00:07:14,230 --> 00:07:16,150
because the built-in heap allocator isn't fast enough for them.

115
00:07:16,160 --> 00:07:19,029
It's another good example.

116
00:07:19,029 --> 00:07:22,150
You mentioned Microsoft's more modern office suites.

117
00:07:22,160 --> 00:07:25,990
I've noticed there are certain applications that aren't supported,

118
00:07:25,990 --> 00:07:30,629
like for example, I think the modern Adobe Creative Suite.

119
00:07:30,629 --> 00:07:32,629
What's the difference with software like that?

120
00:07:32,629 --> 00:07:37,189
And does that also apply to the modern office suite or is that actually supported?

121
00:07:37,189 --> 00:07:41,749
Well, in one case you have things like Microsoft using their own APIs that I mentioned.

122
00:07:41,759 --> 00:07:44,550
With Adobe that applies less, I suppose,

123
00:07:44,560 --> 00:07:46,550
but I think to some degree the answer is.

124
00:07:46,560 --> 00:07:51,350
that some applications are just hard and there's no way around it.

125
00:07:51,350 --> 00:07:55,270
and we can only spend so much time on a hard application.

126
00:07:55,270 --> 00:07:57,510
Debugging things can get very hard with wine.

127
00:07:57,510 --> 00:07:59,670
Let me explain that for a minute.

128
00:07:59,670 --> 00:08:01,510
Because normally when you think about debugging an application,

129
00:08:01,520 --> 00:08:07,029
you say, "Oh, I'm going to open up my debugger, pop it in, break at this point,

130
00:08:07,029 --> 00:08:10,390
see what like all the variables are or if they're not what I expect. or maybe wait for it to crash.

131
00:08:10,390 --> 00:08:14,309
and then get a back trace and see where it crashed and why."

132
00:08:14,309 --> 00:08:16,230
You can't do that with wine because you don't have the application,

133
00:08:16,240 --> 00:08:19,589
you don't have the symbols, you don't have your debugging symbols,

134
00:08:19,589 --> 00:08:23,990
you don't know anything about the code you're running.

135
00:08:23,990 --> 00:08:28,231
unless you take the time to disassemble and decompile and read through it. And that's difficult.

136
00:08:28,231 --> 00:08:31,510
It's only difficult every time I've looked at a program and been like,

137
00:08:31,510 --> 00:08:35,190
I'm going to just try and figure out what the program is doing.

138
00:08:35,190 --> 00:08:38,230
It takes so much time and it is never worth it. And sometimes you have to.

139
00:08:38,230 --> 00:08:42,310
Sometimes you have no other choice.

140
00:08:42,310 --> 00:08:47,350
But usually you rely on seeing what calls it makes into the operating system.

141
00:08:47,350 --> 00:08:49,269
and trying to guess which one of those is going wrong.

142
00:08:49,269 --> 00:08:55,350
Now, sometimes you'll get lucky and it'll crash in wine code.

143
00:08:55,350 --> 00:08:57,030
or sometimes it'll make a call into a function that we don't implement yet.

144
00:08:57,040 --> 00:09:00,710
and we know, oh, we need to implement that function.

145
00:09:00,710 --> 00:09:04,710
But sometimes it does something more obscure and we have to figure out, well,

146
00:09:04,710 --> 00:09:08,230
like all of these millions of calls it made, which one of them are we implementing incorrectly?

147
00:09:08,240 --> 00:09:12,949
So, it's returning the wrong result or not doing something that it should.

148
00:09:12,949 --> 00:09:15,030
And and then you add on to.

149
00:09:15,030 --> 00:09:19,509
that all the sort of harder to debug things like memory errors.

150
00:09:19,509 --> 00:09:22,470
that we could make and it can be very difficult.

151
00:09:22,470 --> 00:09:27,670
And so sometimes some applications just suffer from those hard bugs.

152
00:09:27,670 --> 00:09:32,631
And sometimes it's also just a matter of not enough demand for something for us to spend a. lot of time on it. Right.

153
00:09:32,640 --> 00:09:34,949
Yeah.

154
00:09:34,949 --> 00:09:37,990
I can see how that would be really challenging because like you were saying,

155
00:09:37,990 --> 00:09:39,990
you don't have the symbols, so you don't have the source code.

156
00:09:39,990 --> 00:09:48,870
So, you don't know what any of this software you're supporting, how it was actually written.

157
00:09:48,870 --> 00:09:53,830
And you were saying that a lot of times there may be some behavior that's wrong. or a crash,

158
00:09:53,830 --> 00:09:58,470
but it's not because Wine crashed or there was an error in Wine.

159
00:09:58,470 --> 00:10:00,630
So, you just know the system calls it made,

160
00:10:00,630 --> 00:10:04,790
but you don't know which of the system calls didn't behave the way that the application expected.

161
00:10:04,800 --> 00:10:11,269
Exactly. I can see how that would be really challenging.

162
00:10:11,269 --> 00:10:13,030
And wine runs so many different applications.

163
00:10:13,030 --> 00:10:21,509
I'm kind of curious, how do you even track what's working and what's not as you change wine?

164
00:10:21,509 --> 00:10:23,990
Because if you support thousands or tens of thousands of applications,

165
00:10:24,000 --> 00:10:27,030
how do you know when you've got a regression or not?

166
00:10:27,040 --> 00:10:29,750
Great question.

167
00:10:29,750 --> 00:10:32,470
Probably over half of wine by like source code volume.

168
00:10:32,470 --> 00:10:34,550
I have to actually check what it is,

169
00:10:34,550 --> 00:10:39,190
but I think it's probably over half, is what we call is tests.

170
00:10:39,190 --> 00:10:41,590
And these tests serve two purposes.

171
00:10:41,590 --> 00:10:43,430
The one purpose is they are regression tests.

172
00:10:43,430 --> 00:10:45,590
and the other purpose is they are conformance tests.

173
00:10:45,600 --> 00:10:51,110
that test that test how an API behaves on Windows.

174
00:10:51,110 --> 00:10:53,030
and validates that we are behaving the same way.

175
00:10:53,030 --> 00:10:55,910
So we write all these tests, we run them on Windows.

176
00:10:55,910 --> 00:11:00,150
and write the tests to check what the Windows returns.

177
00:11:00,150 --> 00:11:02,630
and then we run them on Wine and make sure that that matches.

178
00:11:02,640 --> 00:11:09,670
And we have just such a huge body of tests to make sure that we're not breaking anything.

179
00:11:09,670 --> 00:11:14,950
and that all the code that we get into wine that looks like, wow, it's doing that really? Well, nope.

180
00:11:14,959 --> 00:11:16,550
That's what Windows does.

181
00:11:16,550 --> 00:11:19,350
The test says so.

182
00:11:19,350 --> 00:11:21,670
So pretty much any new code that we get,

183
00:11:21,670 --> 00:11:23,269
it has to have tests to demonstrate that it's doing the right thing.

184
00:11:23,279 --> 00:11:29,990
And so rather than testing against a specific application,

185
00:11:29,990 --> 00:11:36,790
seeing if it works, you're making a call to a Windows system call, seeing how it responds,

186
00:11:36,790 --> 00:11:40,470
and then making the same call within Wine and just making sure they match.

187
00:11:40,480 --> 00:11:43,351
Yes. Exactly.

188
00:11:43,351 --> 00:11:45,910
And that is a lot more automatable, right?

189
00:11:45,910 --> 00:11:50,470
Because otherwise you have to manually, you know, these are all graphical applications.

190
00:11:50,470 --> 00:11:54,310
You'd have to manually do the things and make sure they work.

191
00:11:54,310 --> 00:11:58,069
But if you write automatable tests, you can just run them all.

192
00:11:58,069 --> 00:12:00,471
and the machine will complain at you if it fails. It continuous integration.

193
00:12:00,480 --> 00:12:07,430
And because there's all these potential compatibility issues.

194
00:12:07,430 --> 00:12:11,910
where maybe a certain call doesn't behave the way an application expects,

195
00:12:11,910 --> 00:12:17,110
what are the types of ways that shows when someone's using software?

196
00:12:17,110 --> 00:12:18,949
I mean, I think you mentioned crashes,

197
00:12:18,949 --> 00:12:21,430
but I imagine there could be all sorts of other types of behavior.

198
00:12:21,440 --> 00:12:23,750
Yes, very much so.

199
00:12:23,750 --> 00:12:27,990
Basically, anything you can imagine again is what will happen.

200
00:12:27,990 --> 00:12:30,389
You can have crashes are the easy ones.

201
00:12:30,389 --> 00:12:33,750
because you know when and where it crash and you can work backwards from there.

202
00:12:33,750 --> 00:12:36,389
But you can also it could hang.

203
00:12:36,389 --> 00:12:38,390
It could not render right. Like maybe render a black screen.

204
00:12:38,399 --> 00:12:43,030
For games you could very frequently have graphical glitches.

205
00:12:43,030 --> 00:12:47,991
where maybe some objects won't render right or the entire screen will be red. Who knows?

206
00:12:47,991 --> 00:12:51,910
In a very bad case you could even bring down your system.

207
00:12:51,910 --> 00:12:53,670
And we usually say that's not Wine's fault.

208
00:12:53,670 --> 00:12:57,431
That's the graphics library's fault cuz they're not supposed to do that. no matter what we do.

209
00:12:57,431 --> 00:13:02,150
But sometimes we have to work around that anyway.

210
00:13:02,150 --> 00:13:05,430
But yeah, there's been some very strange and idiosyncratic bugs out there, too.

211
00:13:05,440 --> 00:13:07,190
Yeah.

212
00:13:07,190 --> 00:13:10,230
And like you mentioned that there's so many different things.

213
00:13:10,230 --> 00:13:14,950
that could have gone wrong that I imagine it's very difficult to find. Yeah.

214
00:13:14,950 --> 00:13:21,829
And when software runs through wine, I think a lot of our listeners will probably be familiar with.

215
00:13:21,829 --> 00:13:23,430
running things in a virtual machine.

216
00:13:23,430 --> 00:13:28,710
and they know that there's a big performance impact from doing that.

217
00:13:28,710 --> 00:13:38,790
How does the performance of applications compare to running natively on the original Windows OS versus virtual machines?

218
00:13:38,790 --> 00:13:40,629
So in theory, and I haven't actually done this recently,

219
00:13:40,629 --> 00:13:45,670
so I can't speak too much to that, but in theory, the idea is it's a lot faster.

220
00:13:45,680 --> 00:13:51,829
So there is a bit of a joke acronym to Wine.

221
00:13:51,829 --> 00:13:55,350
Wine is not an emulator, even though I start out by saying Wine is an emulator.

222
00:13:55,350 --> 00:13:57,110
and it was originally called a Windows emulator.

223
00:13:57,110 --> 00:14:03,670
But what this basically means is Wine is not a CPU emulator. When you think about emulators in a general sense,

224
00:14:03,670 --> 00:14:10,629
they're often emulators for specific CPUs, often older ones like Commodore emulator or an Omega emulator.

225
00:14:10,629 --> 00:14:14,230
But in this case, you have software that's written for an x86 CPU.

226
00:14:14,240 --> 00:14:20,870
and it's running on an x86 CPU by giving it the same instructions that it's giving on Windows.

227
00:14:20,870 --> 00:14:26,710
It's just that when it says now call this Windows function, it calls us instead.

228
00:14:26,710 --> 00:14:28,790
So that all should perform exactly the same.

229
00:14:28,790 --> 00:14:31,509
The only performance difference at that point is.

230
00:14:31,519 --> 00:14:36,150
that all should perform exactly the same as opposed to a virtual machine.

231
00:14:36,150 --> 00:14:38,389
where you have to interpret the instructions.

232
00:14:38,399 --> 00:14:42,150
and maybe translate them to a different instruction set.

233
00:14:42,150 --> 00:14:48,310
The only performance difference is going to be in the functions that we are implementing themselves.

234
00:14:48,310 --> 00:14:52,629
and we try to implements them to perform as well or almost as well as Windows.

235
00:14:52,629 --> 00:14:55,430
there's always going to be a bit of a theoretical gap.

236
00:14:55,430 --> 00:14:59,670
because we have to translate from say one API to another,

237
00:14:59,670 --> 00:15:02,870
but we try to make that as little as possible.

238
00:15:02,870 --> 00:15:07,509
And in some cases, the operating system we're running on is just better than Windows.

239
00:15:07,509 --> 00:15:12,550
and the libraries we're using are better than Windows,

240
00:15:12,550 --> 00:15:14,870
so our games will run faster, for example.

