00:22:16,470 --> 00:22:20,549
But it's very unusual because we don't ask companies for any of their code.

345
00:22:20,549 --> 00:22:23,990
we don't need it. We just fix the Windows API.

346
00:22:24,000 --> 00:22:26,230
And so in that case, the ports would be.

347
00:22:26,240 --> 00:22:31,029
where let's say someone sells a Mac OS version of their software.

348
00:22:31,029 --> 00:22:37,190
They would bundle crossover with their software, right?

349
00:22:37,190 --> 00:22:40,549
And usually when you do this, it doesn't look like there's crossover there.

350
00:22:40,549 --> 00:22:44,390
Like it just looks like this software is native, but there is crossover under the hood.

351
00:22:44,390 --> 00:22:50,789
And so earlier we were talking about how you're basically intercepting the system calls.

352
00:22:50,799 --> 00:22:58,390
that these binaries are making whether that's the executable or the DLS from Windows.

353
00:22:58,390 --> 00:23:04,230
But I think probably a lot of our listeners are not really sure how that's done.

354
00:23:04,230 --> 00:23:08,310
Like they may have built software but they don't know how do I basically hijack these system calls.

355
00:23:08,310 --> 00:23:10,070
that this application is making.

356
00:23:10,070 --> 00:23:14,549
So maybe you could talk a little bit about how that works.

357
00:23:14,549 --> 00:23:16,230
So there's a couple steps that go into it.

358
00:23:16,230 --> 00:23:20,471
When you think about a program that's a that's a big file that's got all the machine code. in it,

359
00:23:20,471 --> 00:23:24,310
and then it's got stuff at the beginning saying here's how the program works.

360
00:23:24,310 --> 00:23:29,030
and here's where in the file the processor should start running. That's your exe file.

361
00:23:29,030 --> 00:23:34,390
And then then your DLL files are libraries that contain shared code.

362
00:23:34,390 --> 00:23:36,310
and you have I guess similar sort of file.

363
00:23:36,310 --> 00:23:43,190
that says here's the entry point that runs this function this parse XML function or whatever have you.

364
00:23:43,190 --> 00:23:48,870
and here's this entry point that has a generate XML function and so on and so forth.

365
00:23:48,870 --> 00:23:54,950
and then the operating system will basically take the exe file and see all the bits in it.

366
00:23:54,950 --> 00:24:01,029
say I want to call the parse XML function it'll load that DL and hook it up.

367
00:24:01,029 --> 00:24:04,950
so the processor ends up just seeing jump directly to this parse xml function.

368
00:24:04,950 --> 00:24:07,669
and then run that and then return and so on.

369
00:24:07,679 --> 00:24:12,870
And so what wine does is part of wine is a libraries.

370
00:24:12,870 --> 00:24:15,669
that the implementing that parse xml and readxml function.

371
00:24:15,669 --> 00:24:17,669
but part of it is the loader.

372
00:24:17,669 --> 00:24:22,230
which is the part of the operating system that hooks everything together.

373
00:24:22,230 --> 00:24:24,789
and when we load we redirect to our libraries.

374
00:24:24,789 --> 00:24:27,110
We don't have windows libraries we we redirect to ours.

375
00:24:27,120 --> 00:24:32,149
then we run our code and then we jump back to the program.

376
00:24:32,149 --> 00:24:39,190
So it's the loader that's a part of wine that's actually not sure. if running the executable is the right term.

377
00:24:39,200 --> 00:24:40,789
No, I think that's a good term.

378
00:24:40,789 --> 00:24:45,190
It starts in a loader and then we say okay now run the machine code next executable.

379
00:24:45,200 --> 00:24:49,669
and then it runs and it jumps between our libraries and back and so on.

380
00:24:49,669 --> 00:24:56,390
And like you were saying before, often times when it's trying to make a system call,

381
00:24:56,400 --> 00:25:01,029
it ends up being handled by a function that you've written in wine.

382
00:25:01,029 --> 00:25:06,310
and then that in turn will call the Linux system calls.

383
00:25:06,310 --> 00:25:07,990
or the Mac OS system calls to try and accomplish the same result.

384
00:25:08,000 --> 00:25:17,029
Right. Exactly. And something that I think maybe not everyone is familiar with is there's this concept of user space.

385
00:25:17,029 --> 00:25:20,070
versus kernel space. Could you explain what the difference is?

386
00:25:20,080 --> 00:25:25,190
So the way I would describe a kernel is it's the part of the operating system.

387
00:25:25,200 --> 00:25:27,909
that can do anything, right?

388
00:25:27,909 --> 00:25:32,390
So any code that runs on your computer is talking to the processor.

389
00:25:32,390 --> 00:25:35,750
And the processor has to be able to do anything the computer can do.

390
00:25:35,750 --> 00:25:39,029
It has to be able to talk to the hardware.

391
00:25:39,029 --> 00:25:41,190
It has to set up the memory space.

392
00:25:41,190 --> 00:25:43,029
So actually a very complicated task.

393
00:25:43,029 --> 00:25:47,990
Has to be able to switch to another task and basically talk to another program.

394
00:25:47,990 --> 00:25:49,830
And you have to have something there that can do everything.

395
00:25:49,840 --> 00:25:53,029
But you don't want any program to be able to do everything.

396
00:25:53,029 --> 00:25:55,350
Not since the '90s.

397
00:25:55,350 --> 00:25:57,269
It's about when we realized that we can't do that.

398
00:25:57,269 --> 00:25:59,590
So the kernel is the part that can do everything.

399
00:25:59,590 --> 00:26:05,029
And when you need to do something that requires those permissions that you can't give everyone,

400
00:26:05,029 --> 00:26:09,669
you have to talk to the colonel and ask it, hey, can you do this for me, please?

401
00:26:09,669 --> 00:26:13,669
In a very restricted way where it's only the safe things you can do.

402
00:26:13,669 --> 00:26:15,909
And to a degree, it's also like a library, right?

403
00:26:15,909 --> 00:26:23,669
It's kernels have always existed and since they've always just been the core standard library of the computer.

404
00:26:23,669 --> 00:26:28,310
that does the things like read and write files which are very very complicated tasks under the hood.

405
00:26:28,310 --> 00:26:31,990
but look very simple because all you say is write this file and talk to the hardware.

406
00:26:32,000 --> 00:26:36,070
and abstract away all the difference between different drivers.

407
00:26:36,070 --> 00:26:40,070
So the kernel is doing all of these things.

408
00:26:40,070 --> 00:26:43,669
So because the kernel is the part that can do everything,

409
00:26:43,669 --> 00:26:47,510
when you think about the kernel, it is basically one program that is always running on your computer,

410
00:26:47,510 --> 00:26:50,149
but it's only one program.

411
00:26:50,149 --> 00:26:52,710
So when a user calls the kernel,

412
00:26:52,710 --> 00:26:54,630
you are switching from one program to another.

413
00:26:54,630 --> 00:26:59,669
and you're doing a lot of complicated things as part of this. You're switching to the higher privilege level.

414
00:26:59,669 --> 00:27:05,990
where you can do anything and you're switching the state from one program to another.

415
00:27:05,990 --> 00:27:07,669
And so this is what we mean.

416
00:27:07,669 --> 00:27:13,430
when we talk about user space where you're running like a normal program. and kernel space where you've suddenly switched into the kernel.

417
00:27:13,430 --> 00:27:18,630
and now you're executing with increased privileges and a different idea of the process space.

418
00:27:18,630 --> 00:27:22,470
and increased responsibility and so on.

