00:27:37,590 --> 00:27:44,390
No more admin bottlenecks, no add-ons, no BS, just a frictionless platform built for developers.

423
00:27:44,390 --> 00:27:46,470
Try for free at monday.com/dev.

424
00:27:46,480 --> 00:27:55,669
You'll love it.

425
00:27:58,870 --> 00:28:04,870
And so do most applications when you were talking about the system calls for handling 3D audio.

426
00:28:04,870 --> 00:28:09,029
or parsing XML are those system calls considered part of user space.

427
00:28:09,029 --> 00:28:12,870
and then those things call the kernel space on your behalf. or how would you describe that?

428
00:28:12,880 --> 00:28:20,311
So so when you look at Windows most of the Windows library the vast vast majority of it. is all user space.

429
00:28:20,311 --> 00:28:23,510
Most of these libraries that we implement never leave user space.

430
00:28:23,510 --> 00:28:25,669
They never need to call into the kernel.

431
00:28:25,669 --> 00:28:29,590
Only the core low-level stuff, things like we need to read a file, that's a kernel call.

432
00:28:29,590 --> 00:28:33,590
When you need to sleep and wait for some seconds, that's a kernel call.

433
00:28:33,590 --> 00:28:38,710
You need to talk to a different process, things that interact with different processes in general.

434
00:28:38,710 --> 00:28:42,710
Not just allocate memory, but allocate a page of memory like a from the memory manager.

435
00:28:42,710 --> 00:28:44,950
and then that gets suballocated by the heap allocator.

436
00:28:44,960 --> 00:28:47,510
So things like that.

437
00:28:47,510 --> 00:28:53,269
So if I was writing an application and I needed to open a file for example,

438
00:28:53,269 --> 00:28:57,029
does that mean that I would have to communicate with the kernel to read that file?

439
00:28:57,039 --> 00:29:02,310
Right. Exactly. And so most applications it sounds like it's going to be a mixture.

440
00:29:02,310 --> 00:29:10,230
You're going to have a lot of things that call user space calls. and then a few you mentioned more low-level ones.

441
00:29:10,230 --> 00:29:12,789
that are going to require you to communicate with the kernel.

442
00:29:12,799 --> 00:29:15,110
Yeah, basically.

443
00:29:15,110 --> 00:29:18,549
And it's worth noting that in all operating systems,

444
00:29:18,549 --> 00:29:22,789
you're almost always going to be calling a user space library.

445
00:29:22,789 --> 00:29:24,710
that might just be a thin wrapper over the kernel call.

446
00:29:24,710 --> 00:29:27,750
It might it's going to do like just a little bit of work and then call the kernel.

447
00:29:27,750 --> 00:29:31,830
In fact, in Windows, that's the only way to do it.

448
00:29:31,830 --> 00:29:34,310
In many other operating systems, you can.

449
00:29:34,310 --> 00:29:37,269
actually say you can actually tell the processor to make the kernel call.

450
00:29:37,269 --> 00:29:39,830
There is a special instruction that does this.

