676
00:44:38,550 --> 00:44:43,109
get rid of the craft, make it cleaner and make it lower level.

677
00:44:43,109 --> 00:44:47,431
because to get into maximum performance games really want low-level access. And so they made Vulcan.

678
00:44:47,440 --> 00:44:52,630
Microsoft kind of did the same thing, but they still call it Direct 3D.

679
00:44:52,630 --> 00:44:56,871
It's their the newest version of Direct 3D is lower level. It's called Direct 3D 12.

680
00:44:56,880 --> 00:44:59,910
And Mac looked at this and they decided we're going to do the same thing,

681
00:44:59,910 --> 00:45:01,430
too, but we're not going to use Vulcan.

682
00:45:01,430 --> 00:45:05,910
We're going to define our own and they call it metal.

683
00:45:05,910 --> 00:45:14,230
And so when we want to translate D3D12 into something that another operating system understands, that's probably Vulcan.

684
00:45:14,230 --> 00:45:15,990
And on Mac, we need to translate it to Metal somehow.

685
00:45:15,990 --> 00:45:20,870
And we decided instead of having a separate layer from D312 to Metal,

686
00:45:20,870 --> 00:45:23,829
we're just going to translate it to Vulcan and then translate the Vulcan to metal.

687
00:45:23,829 --> 00:45:27,990
And it also lets things written for Vulcan on Windows,

688
00:45:27,990 --> 00:45:31,190
which is also a thing that exists, that lets some work on Metal.

689
00:45:31,200 --> 00:45:40,230
And having to do that translation, does that have a performance impact or is that not really thought?

690
00:45:40,230 --> 00:45:43,349
Yes, it's kind of like anything when you talk about performance.

691
00:45:43,349 --> 00:45:51,589
Like I mentioned this earlier, there's always going to be overhead from translating from one API to another,

692
00:45:51,589 --> 00:45:54,150
but we put in heroic efforts to try to make sure that doesn't matter.

693
00:45:54,160 --> 00:45:59,751
to make sure that stuff that needs to be fast is really as fast as it can possibly. be.

694
00:45:59,751 --> 00:46:02,230
And some very clever things have been done along those lines.

695
00:46:02,230 --> 00:46:06,870
And sometimes the you know the graphics drivers underneath are.

696
00:46:06,870 --> 00:46:10,630
so good that it actually does run better even despite the translation overhead.

697
00:46:10,630 --> 00:46:15,349
And then sometimes to make it run fast we need to say.

698
00:46:15,349 --> 00:46:17,670
well we're going to implement a new API.

699
00:46:17,670 --> 00:46:20,630
that behaves more like Windows so we can do less work translating it.

700
00:46:20,630 --> 00:46:26,150
And sometimes that goes into the graphics library and sometimes that goes into other places.

701
00:46:26,160 --> 00:46:28,790
Yeah.

702
00:46:28,790 --> 00:46:35,109
Something I've found a little bit interesting about the last few years is developers in the past,

703
00:46:35,109 --> 00:46:37,589
they would generally target Windows.

704
00:46:37,589 --> 00:46:43,829
and you might be lucky to get a Mac port or a Linux port.

705
00:46:43,829 --> 00:46:46,470
And I wonder like in your opinion now now.

706
00:46:46,470 --> 00:46:50,309
that a lot of developers are just targeting Windows.

707
00:46:50,309 --> 00:46:53,510
and relying on Wine or or Proton to run their software.

708
00:46:53,520 --> 00:46:56,950
Is there any I suppose downside to doing.

709
00:46:56,960 --> 00:47:02,230
that or is it all just upside like everyone should target Windows as this common platform?

710
00:47:02,240 --> 00:47:03,990
Yeah, it's an interesting question.

711
00:47:04,000 --> 00:47:08,150
There's some people who seem to think it's a bad thing.

712
00:47:08,150 --> 00:47:10,630
that we're not getting native ports in the same sense.

713
00:47:10,630 --> 00:47:15,109
And then there's some people who see, no, that's a perfectly valid way to do ports.

714
00:47:15,109 --> 00:47:17,750
Just write for this de facto common API.

715
00:47:17,750 --> 00:47:23,830
It was never intended as a crossplatform common API, but we've made it one, right? And so why is that any worse than.

716
00:47:23,839 --> 00:47:29,270
if it runs on a different API on Linux or Mac?

717
00:47:29,270 --> 00:47:31,109
And that argument tends to make sense to me.

718
00:47:31,109 --> 00:47:36,470
I don't personally see a lot of reason to say that one library is more pure than another.

719
00:47:36,470 --> 00:47:41,829
Right now, I do think Windows APIs are generally pretty bad.

720
00:47:41,829 --> 00:47:45,670
This might be, you know, just some sort of this might just be an effect of having to.

721
00:47:45,670 --> 00:47:48,550
work with them for a very long time.

722
00:47:48,550 --> 00:47:51,510
and see all their flaws and have to deal with the nonsense that they do.

723
00:47:51,510 --> 00:47:57,589
But I think that a lot of the native Linux APIs are better.

724
00:47:57,589 --> 00:47:59,510
But if you like your Windows API better.

725
00:47:59,510 --> 00:48:05,670
and if you want to target Windows and that's the only way to do it, then sure, why not? What's wrong with that?

726
00:48:05,680 --> 00:48:08,309
Yeah.

727
00:48:08,309 --> 00:48:12,790
And I think doing it this way targeting Windows, I mean, if you look in the past,

728
00:48:12,790 --> 00:48:20,950
even though you had some software that would be ported to other operating systems without this compatibility layer,

729
00:48:20,950 --> 00:48:22,470
without people just targeting Windows,

730
00:48:22,470 --> 00:48:26,870
all this software that people can now run on these portable gaming handhelds or on Linux,

731
00:48:26,880 --> 00:48:29,270
most of that software was never going to be ported.

732
00:48:29,280 --> 00:48:31,110
Oh, yeah. Absolutely.

733
00:48:31,110 --> 00:48:36,309
That's kind of why Wine existed because people wanted to run their software.

734
00:48:36,309 --> 00:48:40,230
that was never going to be ported. They just wanted and Ninja community just spent a lot of effort,

735
00:48:40,240 --> 00:48:42,710
you know, making all these individual programs run.

736
00:48:42,720 --> 00:48:44,470
Yeah.

737
00:48:44,470 --> 00:48:47,990
And I think it's pretty amazing too.

738
00:48:47,990 --> 00:48:51,910
that now that's become this official way I suppose of distributing your software where Yeah.

739
00:48:51,920 --> 00:48:58,470
you say like, "Hey, I made a Windows version, but you're on your Linux machine.

740
00:48:58,470 --> 00:49:03,910
it's officially supported because we have this much belief in this compatibility layer.

741
00:49:03,920 --> 00:49:08,150
It's kind of incredible to see wine having got this far.

742
00:49:08,150 --> 00:49:10,150
I mean, I started working on it,

743
00:49:10,150 --> 00:49:12,470
you know, six, seven years ago and even.

744
00:49:12,470 --> 00:49:15,270
then I could never have imagined it would be like this.

745
00:49:15,270 --> 00:49:18,150
So, as we wrap up, for the developers.

746
00:49:18,150 --> 00:49:23,829
that are listening or or people who are just users of Wine,

747
00:49:23,829 --> 00:49:27,589
is there anything you think they should know about the project that we haven't talked about?

748
00:49:27,599 --> 00:49:29,910
I don't think there's anything I can think of.

749
00:49:29,920 --> 00:49:37,031
And if people want to learn more about the Wine Project or or see what you're up to, where should they head?

750
00:49:37,040 --> 00:49:41,670
We don't really have any things like news, unfortunately.

751
00:49:41,670 --> 00:49:43,670
Read the release notes.

752
00:49:43,670 --> 00:49:46,790
There are some people from codew weavers who do blogs.

753
00:49:46,790 --> 00:49:52,069
So if you go to codereavers.com/blog, there's some code wever stuff uh some marketing stuff,

754
00:49:52,069 --> 00:49:57,030
but there's also some developers who will talk about bugs that they are solving.

755
00:49:57,030 --> 00:49:59,430
and how it's easy and and the experience of working on wine.

756
00:49:59,440 --> 00:50:07,829
And I suppose if someone's interested and like like let's say they have a piece of software,

757
00:50:07,829 --> 00:50:10,069
it's not working through wine,

758
00:50:10,069 --> 00:50:11,990
what's the best place for them to.

759
00:50:11,990 --> 00:50:15,430
either get help or maybe even get involved with trying to fix it?

760
00:50:15,430 --> 00:50:19,349
So you can file a bug on winehq.org.

761
00:50:19,359 --> 00:50:23,750
or find there's a lot of developer resources there.

762
00:50:23,750 --> 00:50:25,829
and you can get involved with contributing to the software.

763
00:50:25,829 --> 00:50:28,069
and there's links to our mailing list.

764
00:50:28,069 --> 00:50:33,670
and IRC channels and the GitLab where all places you can find developers.

765
00:50:33,670 --> 00:50:35,750
We love to help you debug things.

766
00:50:35,750 --> 00:50:37,829
We love to help you fix things.

767
00:50:37,829 --> 00:50:40,390
We try our very best to be a welcoming community.

768
00:50:40,390 --> 00:50:47,671
and we have a lot of experience working with people who want to get their application working. So we'd love to have another.

769
00:50:47,680 --> 00:50:50,790
Very cool.

770
00:50:50,790 --> 00:50:53,430
Yeah, I think wine is a really interesting project.

771
00:50:53,430 --> 00:50:58,950
because I think for I guess it would have been for decades.

772
00:50:58,950 --> 00:51:03,430
it seemed like very niche like not many people Oh yeah.

773
00:51:03,430 --> 00:51:05,910
were aware of it and now I think maybe in particular.

774
00:51:05,910 --> 00:51:10,630
because of the Linux gaming handhelds like the Steam Deck.

775
00:51:10,630 --> 00:51:12,710
Wine is now something that a bunch of people.

776
00:51:12,710 --> 00:51:14,790
who would have never heard about it before now they're aware of it.

777
00:51:14,800 --> 00:51:17,030
Absolutely.

778
00:51:17,030 --> 00:51:20,470
I've watched that transformation happen in real time and it's been surreal.

779
00:51:20,480 --> 00:51:24,630
Very cool. Well, Elizabeth, thank you so much for joining me today.

780
00:51:24,640 --> 00:51:26,870
Thank you, Jeremy. I've been glad to be here.

781
00:51:26,880 --> 00:51:28,630
All right.

